hanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}-1.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}-1 \\\\ x+1 & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+1} \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{x+1})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)>-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>-1,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193013 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186189964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193013 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193013 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193013 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193013 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4c1cf18-a880-5282-8283-771dcb962b4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193013 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ x-3 & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ 3-x & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3-x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{y}}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{4}{3-x} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{4}{3-x})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{64}{(3-x)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{64}{(3-x)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193526 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193526 #edb/ref 1061 39425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193526 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f7338843-a969-5972-9416-842835e163e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193526 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193527 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193527 #edb/ref 1061 39439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193527 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4698c7d-cd57-5e9b-b238-e4552c777ed6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193527 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193528 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193528 #edb/ref 1061 39440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193528 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5533ce0b-1a98-583a-9bde-69357197ab89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193528 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193529 #edb/ref 1061 39441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193529 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d243d211-f610-5e81-93de-952a8c03a5df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193529 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193539 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193539 #edb/ref 1061 39590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193539 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "31d1f629-69a0-5434-a974-b8119146ef70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193539 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193825 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll use our knowledge of solving radical equations to compute inverses of radical functions. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following radical function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\nTo compute the inverse of $f(x),$ we follow the usual four steps:\n\n**Step 1:** First, we replace $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we swap $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = 3\\sqrt{y} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Next, we solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{y}+2 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{y} & =x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}(x-2))}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Finally, we replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x - 2)^2\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f(x) \\geq 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193825 #edb/ref 1065 6511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193825 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "24753060-4992-520a-b948-f4e049858666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193825 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549321 #edb/ref 1080 "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}+1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549321 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549321 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00fd4c22-89d2-5fde-b119-9ce2163bb9d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549340 #edb/ref 1080 "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{64}(x+7)^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549340 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549340 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f5a02a6-edc8-579d-9f3e-8685852c250f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549348 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{25(x-1)^2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549348 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549348 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13479a97-4e44-5471-9c23-134ec902cd16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549352 #edb/ref 1080 "{f}^{-1}(x)=(x+2)^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549352 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549352 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1547820e-1c6e-5874-a693-dbe63b833497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549392 #edb/ref 1080 "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549392 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175921860454